  "stage_result": {
    "thirteen_samples_root_cause": "FOUND AND FIXED. The ring builder forced a minimum radius when local clearance was ~0, and the floor (max(0.0002, ...)) applied REGARDLESS of the scale factor - which is exactly why the failure count was invariant to guide radius 1.0/0.8/0.62/0.45. 13 samples = one ring of 12 vertices plus its centre.",
    "fix_applied": "two changes, both about refusing to place geometry the cavity cannot hold rather than shrinking it forever: (1) centreline points whose local clearance cannot support a ring are dropped and the longest contiguous usable run is kept; (2) any ring that still contributes a failing sample is pruned by index and the guide is rebuilt.",
    "containment_now": {
      "LEFT": "100.0% (1 ring pruned, 425 verification samples)",
      "RIGHT": "100.0% (450 verification samples)"
    },
    "remaining_failure": "minimum clearance is 0.063 mm (6.3e-05 m), against an epsilon of 1.5 mm. Containment passes; clearance does not.",
    "why_clearance_is_small": "the measured clearance AT the centreline points is itself about 0.063 mm, so the path is running almost on the cavity wall rather than through the middle. A mid-wall point in a 5.74 mm wall should show roughly 2.9 mm. The containment result is therefore real but thin: the wall-pair selection inside ordered_crossings() is still picking the wrong interval, most likely a spurious wide interval created by rim faces, instead of the true near/far wall pair.",
    "next_step": "instrument ordered_crossings() to print, for the failing centreline samples, every crossing distance with its epsilon-clustered deduplication, and pick the interval by parity across a SECOND, non-collinear ray rather than by width alone."
  }
